      {
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "output_text",
            "text": "**Elon Musk's xAI was founded in 2023** as an AI company to pursue \"maximum truth-seeking AI\" and compete with OpenAI and Google. It developed the Grok chatbot (with versions like Grok-1.5 in 2024 and later improvements in coding/math), opened Grok to X Premium+ users, open-sourced early models, and pursued massive data centers (e.g., Colossus in Memphis and a major one in Saudi Arabia announced in 2025).[[1]](https://www.reuters.com/technology/musks-xai-launch-improved-version-chatbot-2024-03-29/)\n\nKey later developments include:\n- **March 2025**: xAI acquired X (formerly Twitter), which Musk had bought in 2022, integrating it with Grok and valuing the combined entity highly.[[2]](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-03-28/musk-says-that-his-xai-startup-has-acquired-x-for-33-billion?srnd=homepage-americas)\n- **February 2026**: SpaceX acquired xAI in an all-stock deal, creating a combined company valued at around $1.25 trillion (with plans for a major SpaceX IPO). Musk described it as forming \"the most ambitious, vertically-integrated innovation engine on (and off) Earth,\" combining AI, rockets, Starlink, direct-to-mobile comms, and X. The goal was to enable space-based data centers for cheaper AI compute, potentially powering lunar/Mars expansion.[[3]](https://www.theverge.com/tech/872619/elon-musk-merges-spacex-with-xai-and-x)\n- **Post-merger (February–July 2026)**: xAI underwent reorganization after several co-founder departures (reducing the original 12 to about half/6 remaining). It split into focused teams: Grok (chatbot/voice), coding, Imagine (video generation), and \"Macrohard\" (AI for simulating/automating companies, a playful jab at Microsoft). Musk publicly shared a 45-minute all-hands meeting outlining interplanetary ambitions, including orbital data centers (aiming for 100–200 GW/year, scaling to terawatts), moon-based AI satellite factories, a lunar \"mass driver\" (electromagnetic catapult), self-sustaining lunar cities, and expansion to Mars and beyond. He emphasized truth-seeking AI to avoid dystopian risks and predicted space as the lowest-cost AI compute within 2–3 years.[[4]](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/11/xai-lays-out-interplanetary-ambitions-in-public-all-hands/)\n- **May–July 2026**: xAI was dissolved as a separate entity, becoming the AI division of SpaceX and eventually rebranded as **SpaceXAI** (with a new logo and @SpaceXAI handle). Plans included Starmind space infrastructure demos in 2027 and commercial deployment in 2028.[[5]](https://mashable.com/tech/xai-spacexai-name)\n\nThese announcements reflect Musk's vision of tightly integrating AI with space infrastructure for massive scale. Early coverage focused on the 2023 launch and Grok releases, while 2025–2026 stories center on mergers, restructuring, data center ambitions, and the evolution into SpaceXAI. For the most current details, check official X posts from Musk or @SpaceXAI, as developments have been rapid.",
